# 17. Actual HydraDB implementation

Rename current:

```text
hydra/store.py
```

to something truthful:

```text
lab/projections/sqlite.py
```

Then introduce:

```text
lab/projections/base.py
lab/projections/sqlite.py
lab/projections/hydra.py
```

with:

```python
class LabProjection(Protocol):
    record_worker(...)
    record_version(...)
    record_run(...)
    record_artifact(...)
    record_verification(...)
    record_outcome(...)
    record_learning(...)
    retrieve_brief(...)
    rebuild(...)
```

Hydra implementation uses OpenCypher.

Example projection:

```cypher
MERGE (w:Worker {id: $worker_id})

MERGE (v:WorkerVersion {digest: $manifest})
MERGE (w)-[:HAS_VERSION]->(v)

CREATE (r:Run {
  id: $run_id,
  cost_usd: $cost,
  duration_s: $duration
})

MERGE (v)-[:EXECUTED]->(r)
```

And reuse:

```cypher
MATCH (r:Run)-[:PRODUCED]->(a:Artifact)
MATCH (a)-[:REUSED_IN]->(future:Run)
RETURN a, count(future)
ORDER BY count(future) DESC
```

HydraDB currently provides OpenCypher and graph-native traversal on its object-store-backed architecture, so it fits this derived graph cleanly. ([GitHub][2])

But this invariant is mandatory:

```bash
rm -rf HYDRA_STATE
mwgo lab rebuild
```

must recover everything important.

